name: archives
boss: Clobber
flavor: One > and yesterday's file is gone.
brief: tar bundles many files into one. It does not compress by itself -- -z
brief: pipes the bundle through gzip, -j through bzip2. That is why you see
brief: czf and xzf so often: create/extract, compress, file.
brief:
brief: Always look before you extract. tar tf lists the contents without
brief: writing anything, and an archive that expands into the current
brief: directory instead of a subdirectory will scatter files everywhere.
---
q: %list% what is inside @archive@ without extracting anything
a: tar -tf @archive@
out: .
ref: tar
teach: tar t
teach: tar -f
hint: t lists, and -f names the archive file to read
---
q: %list% the contents of @archive@ verbosely, with sizes and permissions
a: tar -tvf @archive@
out: .
ref: tar
teach: tar -v
hint: v adds the verbose per-entry detail, sizes and permissions
---
q: %make% bundle.tar containing @dir@
a: tar -cf bundle.tar @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar c
hint: c creates a new archive; f names the file to write it to
---
q: %make% a gzip-compressed bundle.tar.gz containing @dir@
a: tar -czf bundle.tar.gz @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar.gz
ref: tar
teach: tar -z
hint: z pipes the archive through gzip as it's written
---
q: %make% a bzip2-compressed bundle.tar.bz2 containing @dir@
a: tar -cjf bundle.tar.bz2 @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar.bz2
ref: tar
teach: tar -j
hint: j pipes the archive through bzip2 instead of gzip
---
q: %get% archive/export.tar into the current directory
a: tar -xf archive/export.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar x
hint: x extracts; with no -C it lands in the current directory
---
q: %get% @archive@ into the @dir@ directory rather than here
a: tar -xzf @archive@ -C @dir@
ref: tar
teach: tar -C
hint: -C changes into that directory before extracting
---
q: {pull one file out of @archive@ and print it to stdout rather than to disk|use tar -O to extract one file from @archive@ straight to stdout|extract src/main.c from @archive@ without writing it, printing it instead}
a: tar -xzOf @archive@ src/main.c
ref: tar
teach: tar -O
hint: O sends the extracted file's bytes to stdout instead of writing it to disk
---
q: %make% bundle.tar from @dir@ but leave out anything ending in .log
a: tar -cf bundle.tar --exclude '*.log' @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar --exclude
hint: --exclude takes a pattern and skips anything that matches it
---
q: %get% @archive@ dropping the first leading directory from every path
a: tar -xzf @archive@ --strip-components 1
ref: tar
teach: tar --strip-components
hint: --strip-components N removes that many leading path segments
---
q: %get% @archive@ overwriting anything already in the way
a: tar -xzf @archive@ --overwrite
ref: tar
teach: tar --overwrite
hint: --overwrite lets extraction replace files already in the way
---
q: %make% bundle.tar from the list of names held in @txt@
a: tar -cf bundle.tar -T @txt@
ref: tar
teach: tar -T
hint: -T reads the list of names to archive from a file instead of the command line
---
q: {bundle @dir@ without descending into its subdirectories|use tar --no-recursion so @dir@'s contents aren't walked into|archive @dir@ as a flat entry, skipping what's inside it}
a: tar -cf bundle.tar --no-recursion @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar --no-recursion
hint: --no-recursion archives a directory entry itself without descending into it
---
q: %get% @archive@ without restoring the permissions it recorded
a: tar -xzf @archive@ --no-same-permissions
ref: tar
teach: tar --no-same-permissions
hint: --no-same-permissions applies your umask instead of the recorded modes
---
q: %get% @archive@ recording numeric owner ids rather than names
a: tar -xzf @archive@ --numeric-owner
ref: tar
teach: tar --numeric-owner
hint: --numeric-owner keeps uid/gid numbers instead of resolving to names
---
q: {compress @txt@ with gzip, replacing it with @txt@.gz|use gzip on @txt@, which becomes @txt@.gz in place|gzip @txt@ so the original is replaced by its .gz}
a: gzip @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.gz
ref: gzip
teach: gzip
hint: gzip replaces the original file with a .gz version by default
---
q: {compress @txt@ but keep the original as well|use gzip -k so @txt@ survives alongside @txt@.gz|gzip @txt@ without deleting the source file}
a: gzip -k @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.gz
ref: gzip
teach: gzip -k
hint: -k keeps the original file alongside the new .gz
---
q: {compress @txt@ to stdout without touching the file|use gzip -c to send compressed output to stdout, redirected to out.gz|pipe @txt@ through gzip -c into out.gz, leaving @txt@ as is}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > out.gz
state: exists:out.gz
ref: gzip
teach: gzip -c
hint: -c writes the compressed bytes to stdout, leaving the file untouched
---
q: {compress @txt@ even if a .gz already exists|use gzip -f to overwrite an existing @txt@.gz|force gzip to replace a .gz that's already there}
a: gzip -f @txt@
ref: gzip
teach: gzip -f
hint: -f overrides gzip's refusal to clobber an existing .gz
---
q: {read a gzipped file without unpacking it to disk|use zcat to print a gzip file's contents without writing anything|stream a .gz file's decompressed contents straight to your terminal}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && zcat z.gz
out: rotate
ref: zcat
teach: zcat
hint: zcat is gzip -dc -- decompress straight to stdout
---
q: {uncompress z.gz back to z|use gunzip to turn z.gz back into z|reverse gzip's compression on z.gz}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && gunzip z.gz
ref: gunzip
teach: gunzip
hint: gunzip is the inverse of gzip, replacing the .gz with the original
---
q: {check that a gzip file is not corrupt, without unpacking it|use gunzip -t to test z.gz for corruption without extracting it|verify z.gz decompresses cleanly, without writing the result}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && gunzip -t z.gz
ref: gunzip
teach: gunzip -t
hint: -t verifies the archive's integrity without writing any output
---
q: {uncompress z.gz to stdout, keeping the file|use gunzip -c so z.gz's contents print without deleting z.gz|stream z.gz's decompressed contents to stdout, file untouched}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && gunzip -c z.gz
out: rotate
ref: gunzip
teach: gunzip -c
hint: -c decompresses to stdout, leaving the .gz file in place
---
q: {compress @txt@ with bzip2 rather than gzip|use bzip2 instead of gzip on @txt@|run bzip2 on @txt@ in place of gzip}
a: bzip2 @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.bz2
ref: bzip2
teach: bzip2
hint: bzip2 works like gzip but usually compresses tighter, at more cpu cost
---
q: {compress @txt@ with bzip2 at the highest compression setting|use bzip2 -9 on @txt@ for maximum compression|run bzip2 at its strongest compression level on @txt@}
a: bzip2 -9 @txt@
ref: bzip2
teach: bzip2 -1..9
hint: -9 is the tightest compression level; -1 is the fastest
---
q: {bzip2 @txt@ to stdout, keeping the original|use bzip2 -c to send compressed output to stdout as out.bz2|pipe @txt@ through bzip2 -c into out.bz2, leaving @txt@ untouched}
a: bzip2 -c @txt@ > out.bz2
state: exists:out.bz2
ref: bzip2
teach: bzip2 -c
hint: -c writes the compressed bytes to stdout instead of replacing the file
---
q: {uncompress a .bz2 file|use bunzip2 to reverse a .bz2 compression|decompress a bzip2 file back to its original}
a: bzip2 -c @txt@ > z.bz2 && bunzip2 z.bz2
ref: bunzip2
teach: bunzip2
hint: bunzip2 is bzip2's inverse, same relationship gunzip has to gzip
---
q: {test a .bz2 file for corruption without unpacking it|use bunzip2 -t to verify a .bz2 file without extracting it|check a bzip2 file decompresses cleanly, without writing the result}
a: bzip2 -c @txt@ > z.bz2 && bunzip2 -t z.bz2
ref: bunzip2
teach: bunzip2 -t
hint: -t tests integrity without writing any decompressed output
---
q: {unpack a zip file into the current directory|use unzip on archive.zip to extract it here|extract every file in archive.zip into the current directory}
a: unzip archive.zip
ref: unzip
teach: unzip
hint: unzip with no flags extracts everything into the current directory
---
q: %list% what is inside a zip without extracting it
a: unzip -l archive.zip
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -l
hint: -l lists contents without extracting anything
---
q: {unpack a zip into a directory you choose|use unzip -d to extract archive.zip into @dir@|extract archive.zip's contents into a directory you name}
a: unzip archive.zip -d @dir@
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -d
hint: -d names the directory to extract into instead of the current one
---
q: {unpack a zip overwriting anything already there, without asking|use unzip -o so existing files are replaced without a prompt|extract archive.zip, forcing overwrite of anything in the way}
a: unzip -o archive.zip
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -o
hint: -o overwrites existing files without prompting for confirmation
---
q: %show% one file out of a zip to stdout instead of writing it
a: unzip -p archive.zip notes.txt
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -p
hint: -p extracts one named file to stdout instead of writing it
---
q: {test a zip for corruption without unpacking it|use unzip -t to verify archive.zip without extracting it|check archive.zip's integrity, writing nothing to disk}
a: unzip -t archive.zip
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -t
hint: -t tests the archive's integrity without extracting anything
